# Retirement gate for src/yuv_bilinear_fix.rs
#
# host:    aarch64-apple-darwin (Darwin 25.5.0), 12 cores
# date:    2026-08-29
# repo:    imazen/zenavif @ f6b49a4c (pre-retirement)
# harness: ~/tmp/yuvfix-parity (standalone; wrapper logic copied verbatim
#          from src/yuv_bilinear_fix.rs so round A reproduces shipped behaviour)
#
# Grid: 8 converters (every variant used at the 8 call sites in src/decoder.rs)
#       x 16 geometries (2x2 .. 2048x2048, both height parities, odd widths)
#       x 2 ranges (Full, Limited)
#       x 3 matrices (Bt601, Bt709, Bt2020)
#       x 3 content classes (gradient, PRNG noise, sharp chroma edges)
#       = 2304 cells per round. Fingerprint = fnv1a64 over the whole output.
#
# Rounds:
#   A       wrapper + yuv =0.8.15   today's shipped behaviour
#   control direct  + yuv =0.8.15   the unrepaired upstream bug
#   B       direct  + yuv =0.8.17   the retirement candidate
#
# RESULTS
#   Anti-vacuity (A vs control):   1836 / 2304 cells DIFFER.
#     The harness demonstrably detects what the wrapper fixes; a pass in the
#     gate below is therefore not vacuous.
#   Gate (A vs B):                 2304 / 2304 cells BYTE-IDENTICAL.
#     Retiring the wrapper against yuv 0.8.17 moves no output byte.
#
#   Mechanism, even-height cells with a written final row (of 1872):
#     control (direct, 0.8.15)      0     <- the bug
#     A       (wrapper, 0.8.15)  1836
#     B       (direct, 0.8.17)   1836     <- upstream now does what the wrapper did
#
#   The 468 cells the wrapper never changed are heights 65 and 257 (odd —
#   upstream has always been complete there, the wrapper passes through by
#   design) plus the h=2 cells, whose final row is legitimately all-zero for
#   the RGB outputs at that content. Identical in A and B either way.
#
# SCOPE NOTE, important for anyone reading this later:
#   All 8 call sites live in src/decoder.rs, which is `#[cfg(feature =
#   "unsafe-asm")]`. Per this repo's CLAUDE.md that feature is compiled by
#   NOTHING - not CI, not the dev box. So the wrapper was dead code in every
#   configuration anyone builds, and this retirement moves no SHIPPED byte
#   regardless. The gate above proves the stronger property - that the code
#   would have been byte-identical had it been live - which is what makes the
#   deletion safe if `unsafe-asm` is ever revived.
